From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winebus: expose Bluetooth DualSense controllers as USB.

With Steam Input disabled, a DualSense
or DualSense Edge connected over Bluetooth is presented to games with the
USB HID report descriptor and report layout, like a wired controller
(PROTON_DUALSENSE_BT_AS_USB=0 disables this):

- extended Bluetooth input report 0x31 is passed on as USB report 0x01
  with the full payload (motion, touchpad, battery); the short report
  sent before the controller switches modes is expanded to the USB layout;
- USB output report 0x02 is sent as Bluetooth report 0x31 with sequence
  number, tag and CRC, so adaptive triggers, lightbar, player LEDs and
  rumble work for games that only speak the USB protocol; compatible
  vibration requests also select the motor emulation (HAPTICS_SELECT and
  vibration v2, as the Linux driver does), since Bluetooth has no haptics
  audio path;
- the device ID uses the USB prefix and the container ID is derived from
  the controller's Bluetooth address, so audio endpoints can be matched
  to it (see winepulse-bluetooth-dualsense-haptics.patch);
- feature reports written by the game get the Bluetooth feature CRC;
  feature report 0x08 is not forwarded, the controller stops responding
  and disconnects after it over Bluetooth (seen with Stellar Blade).
- output reports are coalesced and sent at most every 33 ms: games may
  write them every few milliseconds (Assassin's Creed Shadows animates
  the lightbar at ~400 reports/s), which backs up the Bluetooth link
  next to the haptics audio reports until the controller drops its HID
  connection. In a load test with the speaker stream, 100 state
  reports/s dropped the controller within 5 s, 30/s held. Reports with the same valid flags replace each other and
  are sent in the order of their latest update, so the final state is
  unchanged.

The USB report descriptors and the feature CRC seed follow vDS
(https://github.com/hurryman2212/vds, MIT license).
